From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] SystemUI: the notification swipe decision can be asked on the device

Swiping a notification away does not work on this device. What is measured so
far, on V82, with a clearable notification (the screenshot one, flags=0x10):

  * it never dismisses -- 150, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 900 and 1200 ms across
    850-1040 px, both directions, screen awake, row bounds read from
    `uiautomator dump` before each try. The row translates a little and snaps
    back every time;
  * the same `input swipe` in Recents **does** dismiss its card;
  * 「全部清除」 removes the same notification, so nothing is wrong with the
    removal path itself;
  * one MOVE was caught in `dumpsys input` with `age=189.3ms, wait=187.7ms`,
    so the shade takes about that long per event while dragging;
  * a gesture slow enough that no MOVE is processed within 750 ms
    (`ViewConfiguration.getLongPressTimeout() * 1.5`) opens the notification's
    guts instead, because `removeLongPressCallback()` only runs once dragging
    has begun.

The decision itself is three predicates in `NotificationMenuRow.handleUpEvent`
and `SwipeHelper`: `swipedFarEnough()` compares the **view's** translation
against 0.4 of its width (429 px here, against 196 px for a Recents card,
which is one reason the two behave differently), `swipedFastEnough()` compares
the velocity tracker against 500 dp/s and requires its sign to match that same
translation, and `canChildBeDismissed()` has to hold. On a panel whose frames
take 134-463 ms the view's translation is not the finger's travel, so any of
the three can be the one that fails, and from outside they are
indistinguishable: all three produce the same snap-back.

This class already prints every one of those values, and the next line prints
the outcome -- `mTranslation`, `velocity`, `minDismissVel`, `isDismissGesture`,
`gestureTowardsMenu`, `gestureFastEnough`, `gestureFarEnough`. They are behind
a compile-time `false`, so the only way to ask the question is another system
image. `Log.isLoggable` is the form the rest of the platform uses for exactly
this, it reads one system property at class load and costs nothing after, and
it leaves the build identical for anyone who does not set the tag:

    adb shell setprop log.tag.swipe DEBUG     # before SystemUI starts
    adb shell killall com.android.systemui    # the flag is read at class load

This patch is the question, not the answer. The fix goes in once the values
say which predicate is wrong -- and the answer is expected to be that the
decision has to come from the touch events rather than from a view that cannot
keep up, which is rule 6 of docs/design/eink-ui.md applied to this gesture.

--- a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/statusbar/NotificationMenuRow.java
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/statusbar/NotificationMenuRow.java
@@ -48,8 +48,15 @@
 public class NotificationMenuRow implements NotificationMenuRowPlugin, View.OnClickListener,
         ExpandableNotificationRow.LayoutListener {
 
-    private static final boolean DEBUG = false;
     private static final String TAG = "swipe";
+    // PW3: this class already prints every value the dismiss decision is made
+    // from -- translation, velocity, and each of the three predicates. On a
+    // screen whose frames take 134-463 ms the view cannot keep up with the
+    // finger, so which of those values is wrong is the whole question, and a
+    // compile-time false means the only way to ask is another system image.
+    // Log.isLoggable costs nothing when the tag is not set: adb shell setprop
+    // log.tag.swipe DEBUG.
+    private static final boolean DEBUG = Log.isLoggable(TAG, Log.DEBUG);
 
     private static final int ICON_ALPHA_ANIM_DURATION = 200;
     private static final long SHOW_MENU_DELAY = 60;
 